<body>
  <h1>BTC Cycle Real Account Tracking System</h1>

  <p><strong>Document purpose:</strong> This is the plain-language operating manual for tracking the Bitcoin four-year-cycle system studied in the BTC_cyc workbook. It is designed for real-account monitoring, daily review, and a future GitHub dashboard.</p>

  <p><strong>Primary system:</strong> Bitcoin four-year-cycle timing using the halving date as the known calendar anchor, with an earlier entry intended to approximate the bottom-to-top cycle clock.</p>

  <div class="rule">
    Trading rule: buy BTC 500 days before the Bitcoin halving date and hold through 540 days after the halving date. Hold cash outside that window.
  </div>

  <h2>1. Strategy Summary</h2>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Item</th>
      <th>Rule</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Invested asset</td>
      <td>BTC, represented by BTC-USD or a real Bitcoin holding.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Defensive asset</td>
      <td>Cash or Treasury-like cash equivalent. The backtest treats this as cash with no interest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Signal source</td>
      <td>Bitcoin halving date, plus fixed pre-halving and post-halving day counts.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Buy / hold window</td>
      <td>From 500 days before halving through 540 days after halving, inclusive.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Sell / cash window</td>
      <td>Cash before the pre-halving buy date and after day 540 following the halving.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Current 2024-cycle anchor</td>
      <td>Halving date: April 20, 2024. Buy date: December 7, 2022. Day 540 sell date: October 12, 2025.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Current status as of August 1, 2026</td>
      <td>Outside the 2024-cycle holding window. The model status is Hold Cash.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>2. Exact Operating Rules</h2>

  <h3>Entry Rule</h3>
  <p>Enter BTC 500 calendar days before the confirmed Bitcoin halving date. For the 2024 cycle, the halving date was April 20, 2024, so the model entry date was December 7, 2022.</p>

  <h3>Exit Rule</h3>
  <p>Exit BTC after day 540 following the halving date and move the strategy sleeve to cash. For the 2024 cycle, day 540 was October 12, 2025, so the first cash day was October 13, 2025.</p>

  <h3>Re-entry Rule</h3>
  <p>Remain in cash after the exit date until the next cycle's pre-halving buy date, which is 500 days before the next confirmed Bitcoin halving date. When the next halving date estimate changes materially, recalculate the buy and sell dates.</p>

  <h3>Cycle Clock Interpretation</h3>
  <p>This revised rule is intended to approximate the bottom-to-top day-count framework without needing to identify the exact bottom in real time. Historically, the halving has landed roughly 500-plus days after the prior bear-market bottom, so buying 500 days before halving places the entry near the early bottom-to-top window, while selling 540 days after halving lands near the historical top zone around day 1,050 to day 1,080 after the prior bottom.</p>

  <h3>Bottom-To-Top Day-Count Mapping</h3>
  <p>Using the bottom as day 1, the revised rule maps closely to the observed bottom-to-top cycle clock. The model does not need to know the exact bottom date in real time; the bottom dates below are used to explain why the halving-based rule approximates that framework.</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Cycle</th>
      <th>Observed / Assumed Bottom</th>
      <th>Halving Date</th>
      <th>Buy 500 Days Before Halving</th>
      <th>Buy Day On Bottom Clock</th>
      <th>Sell 540 Days After Halving</th>
      <th>Sell Day On Bottom Clock</th>
      <th>Top Zone</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2015 to 2017</td>
      <td>January 14, 2015</td>
      <td>July 9, 2016</td>
      <td>February 25, 2015</td>
      <td>About day 43</td>
      <td>December 31, 2017</td>
      <td>About day 1,083</td>
      <td>Around day 1,067</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2018 to 2021</td>
      <td>December 15, 2018</td>
      <td>May 11, 2020</td>
      <td>December 28, 2018</td>
      <td>About day 14</td>
      <td>November 2, 2021</td>
      <td>About day 1,054</td>
      <td>Around day 1,059</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2022 to 2025</td>
      <td>November 21, 2022</td>
      <td>April 20, 2024</td>
      <td>December 7, 2022</td>
      <td>About day 17</td>
      <td>October 12, 2025</td>
      <td>About day 1,057</td>
      <td>Around day 1,062</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>No Override Indicators</h3>
  <p>The 200-week moving average was studied, but it is not the primary real-account trigger for this system. The earlier workbook showed that the halving-cycle day 0-540 rule beat the 200-week-SMA rule in that test, but the current operating rule has now been updated to the wider 500-days-before-halving through 540-days-after-halving window.</p>

  <h2>3. Current-Cycle Calendar</h2>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Milestone</th>
      <th>Date</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2024 halving</td>
      <td>April 20, 2024</td>
      <td>Halving anchor. The model was already holding BTC because entry was 500 days earlier.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2024-cycle buy date</td>
      <td>December 7, 2022</td>
      <td>500 days before the April 20, 2024 halving. Model enters BTC.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Day 540</td>
      <td>October 12, 2025</td>
      <td>Last day of the tested BTC holding window.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>First cash day</td>
      <td>October 13, 2025</td>
      <td>Model exits BTC and holds cash.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Estimated bottom window from prior study</td>
      <td>Early to late October 2026</td>
      <td>Useful for planning gradual accumulation, but this is not a formal signal in the tested rule.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Next halving</td>
      <td>To be confirmed by network block height</td>
      <td>The next formal re-entry point for the tested BTC cycle system.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>4. Backtest Results And Rule Status</h2>

  <p>The earlier BTC-only comparison came from <strong>Bitcoin_strategy_comparison.csv</strong> and <strong>Bitcoin_4YearCycle_vs_200WeekSMA.xlsx</strong>. Those historical results tested the prior halving-to-day-540 rule. The revised 500-days-before-halving entry rule should be treated as the current operating rule, with a fresh backtest still needed for exact performance statistics.</p>

  <div class="note">
    Rule update rationale: the bottom-to-top model captured more of the historical bull move because it bought much earlier than the halving date. The revised rule keeps the halving date as the known anchor, but shifts entry 500 days earlier so the holding window better approximates the bottom-to-top cycle without requiring exact real-time bottom identification.
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Strategy</th>
      <th>Test Window</th>
      <th>Final Value From $1,000</th>
      <th>CAGR</th>
      <th>Max Drawdown</th>
      <th>Notes</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Cycle window day 0-540</td>
      <td>May 11, 2014 to June 10, 2026</td>
      <td>$320,703.29</td>
      <td>61.22%</td>
      <td>-53.08%</td>
      <td>Prior tested BTC-only rule. Superseded as the current operating rule by the 500-days-before-halving entry rule.</td>
    </tr>
